  "the_leniency": {
    "reading": "Poisson closed a one-voxel gap between two boxes. The surface it put there corresponds to nothing in the reference, and every point on it is within a voxel of the two facing walls, so unsigned distance to the whole reference cannot separate it from the walls themselves.",
    "reproduces": true,
    "what_the_current_measure_says_about_the_bridge": 0.0
  },
  "what_this_measures": "Four readings of two Poisson reconstructions. The bridged one reconstructs two boxes a single voxel apart, which Poisson fuses across the gap on its own; the surface spanning that gap is fabricated, because the reference has nothing there. The faithful one reconstructs a single box, where nothing is fabricated.",
  "which_replacement_catches_it": {
    "area_inside_the_reference_is_always_a_subset_of_the_far_area": true,
    "both": 0.004601645296945416,
    "normal_correspondence": 0.004601645296945416,
    "signed_distance": 0.0,
    "signed_distance_equals_unsigned_in_every_case": true,
    "why_signed_distance_is_not_enough": "It is redundant here rather than inapplicable, which is a stronger result and not what was assumed before measuring. Surface inside the reference does exist in the comb cases, but every point of it is also more than a voxel from the reference, so the inside set is a subset of the far set the unsigned measure already had. Adding a signed test changes the reading by exactly zero in all ten cases here. The runner named signed distance and per-region correspondence as candidates; only the second one moves this measure."
